                  {
                      "parameters":  {
                                         "content":  "\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  # 04 — Workflow Error Handler (Overview)\n\n  **What it does:** Shared catch-all for unexpected failures in Workflows 01, 02, and 03 (assigned as their error workflow). Captures the failed execution, stores one sanitized incident\n  row in the `workflow_errors` Data Table, and sends a best-effort Gmail alert to the owner. It handles only unexpected failures — expected business outcomes (no topics, disapprovals,\n  rejected callbacks) stay inside the calling workflows.\n\n  **Important process:**\n  `01. Trigger — Workflow Error` (Error Trigger) → `02. Normalize — Incident` (whitelisted fields only) → `03. Insert — workflow_errors` (upsert by `dedupe_key`) → `04. Send — Email Alert`\n  (Gmail)\n\n  **Output:**\n  - One `workflow_errors` row per incident (dedupe key = `workflow_id:execution_id`): `timestamp`, `workflow_id`, `workflow_name`, `execution_id`, `execution_url`, `failed_node`,\n  `error_name`, `error_message`, `execution_mode`, `status` (`open`), `dedupe_key`.\n  - `error_message` is a fixed sanitized string (\"Unhandled workflow error. Open the linked execution for details.\") — no credentials, tokens, payloads, or stack traces are stored or\n  emailed.\n  - Gmail alert to `[ADD YOUR EMAIL ADDRESS]` with workflow name/ID, failed node, error name, execution mode, execution link, timestamp, and incident key.\n\n  **Error handling:**\n  - Data Table write happens before the email, so a failed email can never erase the stored incident.\n  - Gmail is best-effort: 3 attempts with 5s waits and `continueRegularOutput`, so alerting failure does not turn into another error.\n  - Gmail is a different channel than the Telegram-based workflows, avoiding recursive notification loops.\n  - Reruns of the same failed execution update the existing row instead of creating duplicates.",
                                         "height":  816,
                                         "width":  1424,
                                         "color":  "#070D12"
                                     },
